{
  "title": "Interrupted test orchestration can leave process-compose children \
running",
  "description": "An interrupted or timed-out `make test` can leave helper \
processes from `test-compose.yaml` running after the parent process-compose \
process is gone. Observed orphaned `python3 tests/mock_openrouter.py ...`, \
`python3 ../fixture_server.py ...`, and `./target/release/ramekin-server` \
processes reparented to PID 1. Later `make test` runs can then bind-fail on \
the configured `test.env` ports, but still appear to make progress by talking \
to the stale server on those ports.\n\
\n\
This appears to be in our orchestration scripts rather than intentional \
process-compose usage. `scripts/run-tests.sh` and `scripts/run-ui-tests.sh` \
run `process-compose up ...` in the foreground with no cleanup trap around it. \
`scripts/repo-lock.sh` installs a trap, but that trap only releases the repo \
lock and does not stop process-compose or its child processes. The fix should \
add robust INT/TERM/EXIT cleanup around process-compose test orchestration, \
and should avoid cross-worktree cleanup by using the configured \
`PROCESS_COMPOSE_PORT` and/or cwd-scoped process checks. Add tests for the \
interrupted-run path if practical.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 3,
  "type": "bug",
  "labels": [
    "tests",
    "dev-infra",
  ],
